#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/id #uuid "001529aa-c97a-5d69-82fb-cd4319a4dd7d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3585" :question/problem "For the function $y=f(x)$ below, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/1a/1aeebec4df41080efc0d91a8adf00a799828e793e731ed7a36d32d881110596b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has discontinuities due to vertical asymptotes at $x=\\pm 1$. Note that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}} f(x) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=-1$. Similarly\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1^{-}} f(x) = \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich is enough to conclude that there is a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote at $x=1$.\n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-2$, but this is a point discontinuity, not a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129090 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976f6e20-2c45-5123-bb60-738e099facc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "58f2bf5b-04b1-5931-8452-c095b55722ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-3586" :question/problem "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-5x+6}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expression in the denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (1)/((x - 3)(x - 2))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we can find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator to zero:\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x-2) = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=2,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has infinite discontinuities at $x=2$ and $x=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129091 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea5bdd3b-810c-56e6-acfd-4de0e0cfc4e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of Rational Functions With Common Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "26d318a3-0b3f-50be-9802-92b10b83d119" :question/math-academy-id "e-3587" :question/problem "Find all the points where the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2+x-6}{x^2-4}$ has infinite discontinuities." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, let's start by factoring the expressions in the numerator and denominator. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x^{2} + x - 6}{x^{2} - 4} \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= ((x - 2)(x + 3))/((x - 2)(x + 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x + 3}{x + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx+2 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129092 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4317f5b8-cc60-5695-b5bb-47744cecc4cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Infinite Discontinuities of a Piecewise Function"}] :question/id #uuid "83f4e413-e2f4-53dd-9893-fdf8e2df2642" :question/math-academy-id "e-3588" :question/problem "Find all of the points where $f(x)$ below has infinite discontinuities.\n\n${\\frac{1}{x - 5},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x - 4},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 5$." :question/worked-solution "Infinite discontinuities coincide with the vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x)$. The only possible way that $y=f(x)$ can have vertical asymptotes is if either\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\ngrow without bound within their respective subdomains.\n\n- We first compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-5}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-5=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=5$.\nAlthough this branch of the function is defined for $x < 5$, we do have that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 5^{-}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x-5}\\right) = -\\infty$,\nand therefore we conclude that $x=5$ is indeed an infinite discontinuity.\n- Next, we compute the asymptotes of $\\dfrac{1}{x-4}$. Setting the denominator equal to zero, we get\n$x-4=0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=4$.\nHowever, this branch of the function is defined for $x \\geq 5$, which does not contain the neighborhood of $x=4$. So $x=4$ cannot be a vertical asymptote, and therefore $x=4$ cannot be an infinite discontinuity.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ only has a single infinite discontinuity, and it is at $x=5$.\n\nThis matches up with what we see in the graph of $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c1e68cf3a87e981a07fdf6c8a2816ed25b26670b8afa274d95f05e7ea90ee340.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3170b49-74e4-540e-bd64-1be5e84605d3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Discontinuities Due to Vertical Asymptotes Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9530401c-ab1e-5275-a7b8-d8c99543acc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127712} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4fafd5-5e35-5a68-a61b-4962e040f75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127713} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e836-2009-5a91-bfbb-c9ce7fd7e626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ and $x = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127714} {:answer/id #uuid "e9363bc0-9720-5815-8778-a9279ad7fa8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127715} {:answer/id #uuid "634c85b3-8ba0-5c3e-8f34-c077b0971d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ and $x =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127716}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6bec9c-e43c-5c8c-a9dd-a2c9d29fe6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127711}] :question/id #uuid "41d68181-b01b-50ec-a85b-6bd02941be55" :question/math-academy-id "q-38909" :question/problem "![](images/74/744bb33e33eb84d02f53403b9f6ccd6077f6f9a09b5781ffdc31f61c6b5b8343.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x)$ above, where does the function have a discontinuity due to a vertical asymptote?"}]}
